**END OF DESIGN. No experiment was performed; this document is the complete pre-registered protocol to be executed only after the provisioning blockers in §5 are resolved and the [ASSUMPTION] items in §6 are confirmed or explicitly accepted as assumptions by the researcher.**", "evidence": "Workspace exploration (executed, design-only): /workspace contains only task.json (491 B); its documents field = "track.md: Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required." Full-filesystem searches (find for track.md, *repobench*, *.md/.csv/.jsonl/.json excluding system dirs) returned only /workspace/task.json. Environment verified: python3 3.14.4 (/usr/bin/python3); importlib.util.find_spec returns False for torch, transformers, datasets, numpy, pandas, git, hf; environment reports network disabled, gpu_enabled false, cpu_affinity_count 224; free -h shows ~2 TB RAM; df shows a 1 TB mount at /workspace (99% used). No datasets, model weights, or ML libraries present anywhere. These observations are consistent with a design-only task; no experiment was run.", "assumptions": ["ASSUMPTION-1: Official RepoBench Python test split exists and can be provisioned offline; otherwise a seeded split of the full Python set is used and flagged.", "ASSUMPTION-2 (highest impact): cross_file_first sample = gold cross-file context (official order) + target-file prefix to a line-boundary marker; label = next gold line(s); cross_file_random = matched random-context control. To be locked to the official format when available.", "ASSUMPTION-3: Official scoring = Exact Match on next line(s) + Edit Similarity; no execution component in the benchmark's code-completion contract.", "ASSUMPTION-4: RepoBench prompt formatting convention (header lines, blank-line separators, marker placement) mirrored from official eval script; currently the protocol's own locked convention pending that source.", "ASSUMPTION-5: 'Next line' scope = 1 line primary / 3 lines secondary with explicit stop/newline handling; default 1-line primary pending researcher confirmation.", "ASSUMPTION-6: Off-the-shelf pretrained gpt2 and codellama/CodeLlama-7b-hf, no fine-tuning, define the factor 'size + code specialization'.", "ASSUMPTION-7: The <=1024-token fitness filter keeps inputs byte-identical for both models; induced coverage narrowing is a documented, reported tradeoff and results are scoped to GPT-2-feasible contexts.", "ASSUMPTION-8: 'Performance' is operationally defined as text-match metrics (EM primary, Edit Similarity secondary); execution-based pass@k correctness is out of scope.", "ASSUMPTION-9: CPU-only CodeLlama-7B inference for ~450 samples is feasible (est. ~1-8h wall clock across 224 cores); Python 3.14 torch/transformers wheel compatibility is unverified and may require a provisioned compatible environment."], "uncertainties": ["Exact official prompt separators/template for RepoBench (not confirmed by any document).", "Exact normalization in the official scorer (whitespace/indent, set-based vs exact-string, 1-line vs 3-line target).", "Eligibility rate of the test split under the 1024-token filter (unknown until data provisioned).", "Achievable CodeLlama-7B CPU token throughput (top execution/feasibility risk; measured in the N=50 qualifier phase).", "Python 3.14 wheel availability for torch/transformers; may force a provisioned alternate environment.", "Tree-sitter/sacrebleu availability for optional CodeBLEU and static line-type labeling (dependency-gated tertiary metrics)."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-e83ccb5094eb43ce971dfed1c3ee0943", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-e83ccb5094eb43ce971dfed1c3ee0943", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{"answer": "# FINAL EXPERIMENT DESIGN (deliverable)\n\n**STATUS: Design specification only. No experiment has been executed. All compute-, data-, and result-related statements below are a protocol to be followed in a future run, not observations. Statements labeled [ASSUMPTION] are external-knowledge claims about RepoBench (Liu et al., ICLR 2024; github.com/amazon-science/repo-benchmark) that are NOT confirmed by any provided document; each is paired with a validation step or flag in Section 6.**\n\n---\n\n## 1. Rationale and hypotheses\n\n**Rationale.** RepoBench tests repository-level code completion where a model must continue a target file using cross-file context. Comparing a small general-purpose LM (GPT-2 small, 124M, byte-level BPE trained on English text, 1024-token window) with a 7B code-specialized LM (CodeLlama-7b, SentencePiece/Llama tokenizer, 16k window, pretrained on code) isolates the joint factor \"capacity + code specialization.\" CodeLlama is expected to dominate on surface-level code fluency and on using cross-file context, but a controlled measurement (identical inputs, decoding, and scoring) is needed to (i) quantify the gap, (ii) test whether the gap is really driven by cross-file context usage vs simple memorization of Python scaffolding, and (iii) characterize where (which line types / context sizes) each model fails.\n\n**Pre-specified hypotheses (falsifiable, directional):**\n- **H1 (primary):** CodeLlama-7b achieves higher Exact Match (EM) than GPT-2 small on RepoBench Python `cross_file_first` next-line completion on the evaluable subset (paired, one-sided, \u03b1=0.05).\n- **H2:** CodeLlama-7b achieves higher Edit Similarity than GPT-2 small (paired, \u03b1=0.05).\n- **H3 (context-use test):** the `cross_file_first` minus random-context (control) delta in EM is larger for CodeLlama than for GPT-2, i.e., CodeLlama derives more benefit from the gold cross-file context (interaction test; H3 is secondary/exploratory).\n- **H4 (exploratory):** the relative gap is smaller for short-context, single-line targets and larger for long-context or structural targets (e.g., `return`/control-flow lines after imports), consistent with GPT-2's short-window and weak code induction limits.\n\n**Operational definition of \"performance.\"** Performance = text-match code-completion quality: Exact Match (primary) and Edit Similarity (secondary) on the next line(s), computed identically for both models. **Execution-based `pass@k`/runtime-correctness is explicitly out of scope**: no execution sandbox, no test harness, and the benchmark's code-completion contract is text-based [ASSUMPTION-3]. Results will be worded as \"predicted-text matches the gold next line exactly / approximately,\" never as \"code runs.\"\n\n---\n\n## 2. Full protocol\n\n### 2.1 Data, split, subsampling, filtering\n- **Source [ASSUMPTION-1]:** official RepoBench Python `cross_file_first` test split (must be provisioned offline; see \u00a75). Repository/data files are not present in the workspace and must be supplied before any run.\n- **Qualifier phase:** draw a seeded **N=50** sample and run the entire pipeline end-to-end; verify (a) prompt assembly parses, (b) per-model token budgets are respected, (c) scorer reproduces EM/Edit-Sim on hand-checked cases. Fix protocol bugs here; do not report these 50 in final results.\n- **Fitness filter (applied to test split):** an example is eligible iff the FULL assembled prompt (context files + target prefix + marker, see \u00a72.2) fits within **GPT-2's 1024-token window** using GPT-2's tokenizer. This guarantees both models see byte-identical complete text (no truncation \u2192 removes truncation as a confound). Record the eligibility rate and (for the analysis \u00a74.5) the difficulty-coverage tradeoff this induces.\n- **Final sample:** from eligible examples, **N=400** drawn with **seeded stratified sampling**: stratify by repository, draw proportionally, cap per-repo. Seeded across two seeds (0 and 2026) as a robustness pair; primary report = seed 0, seed 2026 used in \u00a74.5 sensitivity.\n- **Control condition (same 400 samples):** the \"no/random context\" variant uses the same target-prefix/marker but with the cross-file context REPLACED by a matched-size set of random files from the same repo (`cross_file_random`-style) [ASSUMPTION-2]. This is an ablation of context usefulness, not an extra dataset.\n\n### 2.2 Prompt assembly for `cross_file_first` [ASSUMPTION-2,4]\nAssembled prompt template (identical for both models; only per-model token budget checks differ):\n```\n<path:context_file_1>\n<text of context_file_1>\n\n<path:context_file_k>\n<text of context_file_k>\n\n<path:target_file.py>\n<target file text up to the line-boundary marker>MARKER_START\n<target line(s) to generate>MARKER_END\n```\n- **Context-selection rule:** use the example's `cross_file_first` context exactly as provided by the benchmark [ASSUMPTION-2]; preserve the official file ordering (no re-ranking) to avoid introducing our own retrieval into the comparison. Number of context files and total context size are recorded per example for the error breakdown (\u00a74.4).\n- **Separators:** a header line `<path:...>` above each file, blank line between files (mirrors the benchmark's stated concatenation convention; exact separators to be locked to the official eval script when available \u2014 flagged, see \u00a76).\n- **Marker:** single line-boundary sentinel placed immediately before the first gold target line; the model never sees gold text past the marker.\n- **Token budgets:** GPT-2 budget = 1024 tokens (its full window); CodeLlama budget = 16,384 tokens. The fitness filter (\u00a72.1) ensures both budgets are satisfied with no clipping. `max_new_tokens` is computed from the gold target block (see \u00a72.3), not from a fixed count.\n\n### 2.3 Model and decoding configuration\n**Shared across both models:** off-the-shelf pretrained checkpoints, **no fine-tuning** (factor = pretraining + size only). Greedy decoding (temperature=0, `do_sample=False`) as the primary configuration. `pad_token_id` set explicitly; generation batched (see \u00a75).\n- **GPT-2 small (`gpt2`, 124M):** its byte-level BPE tokenizer; window 1024; prompts must pass the fitness filter.\n- **CodeLlama-7b (`codellama/CodeLlama-7b-hf`, ~7B):** its Llama SentencePiece tokenizer; window 16,384; same prompt strings, no reformatting.\n- **`max_new_tokens`:** primary = tokens needed for **1 gold line** (computed with the generating model's own tokenizer from the gold reference, so each model emits \"one target line\"); secondary = **3 gold lines**. No sampling in primary runs. Stop at the newline(s) terminating the gold line block per the contract [ASSUMPTION-2,5].\n- **Precision/loading:** bf16 (fallback fp32) on CPU; no quantization in primary runs (a quantization sensitivity run is optional, \u00a74.5, to check robustness of the 7B results).\n\n### 2.4 Evaluation pipeline\n1. Generate greedy continuations from the marker for both models on exactly the same 400 prompts (plus 50 qualifier).\n2. Extract the generated block: text from after the marker up to the configured stop (1-line or 3-line condition).\n3. **Normalization (identical for both models, applied to model output and reference):** strip trailing newlines/whitespace; normalize line endings to `\\n`; do NOT dedent or strip leading indentation (indentation is part of matching); do NOT strip the final partial line.\n4. **Metrics (code-tokenization-free; computed on strings, so tokenizer mismatch cannot distort scoring):**\n   - **EM** (primary): exact string equality of normalized generated block vs normalized gold block \u2192 0/1 per sample. Also reported as **line-set EM** (all generated lines matched, order-preserved) for the 3-line condition.\n   - **Edit Similarity** (secondary): 1 \u2212 (Levenshtein / max(len(ref), len(gen))) on the normalized block.\n   - Optional tertiary: character-level F1 and CodeBLEU (needs a Python tokenizer such as tree-sitter \u2014 flagged as optional/dependency-gated).\n5. Every result row stores the per-model generated text so all metrics, breakdowns, and error audits are reproducible from the same artifacts.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Controls and threat mitigation\n\n| Threat | Mitigation |\n|---|---|\n| **Truncation confound** (GPT-2 window < long contexts) | Fitness filter guarantees byte-identical full prompts for both models in the primary analysis; separate \u00a74.5 truncation-sensitivity check front-clips context to a shared token budget and reports the delta. |\n| **Difficulty-coverage tradeoff** (filter keeps only \u22641024-token prompts) | Reported explicitly: (i) eligibility rate of the test split, (ii) comparison of sample vs full-split difficulty using GPT-2's own performance as a proxy where computable, (iii) length strata in breakdown \u00a74.4. No claim is made that results generalize to longer-context examples. |\n| **Prompt-formatting artifacts** (both models merely parrot the template) | Random/no-context control condition (\u00a72.1, H3): if a model's `cross_file_first` gain over its own control is ~0, its absolute score is treated as format-driven, not cross-file-driven. |\n| **Tokenizer mismatch** (byte-BPE vs SPM; different token counts) | (i) Metrics computed on normalized strings, never on tokens; (ii) token budgets computed per model; (iii) text is byte-identical; (iv) 7B window advantage is neutralized by the fitness filter in the primary design. |\n| **Decoding stochasticity** | Primary = greedy (deterministic, seed-fixed). Sampling (T=0.2, top-p=0.9) as sensitivity (\u00a74.5) with 5 seeds reported as median\u00b1spread, never conflated with greedy results. |\n| **Context usefulness** (does the model actually use it?) | `cross_file_first \u2212 control` delta per model (\u00a72.1, H3); also error audit that flags samples where predictions equal control predictions (no context effect). |\n| **Scorer/implementation bias** | Identical scorer binary applied to both models; normalization locked before any run on the qualifier set; hand-audited on 10 hand-constructed cases during qualifier. |\n| **Data/selection bias** | Seeded stratified sampling; two seeds; pre-registered N, filter, and analyses (all in this document). |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Analysis plan\n\n### 4.1 Point estimates and CIs\nPer model, per condition (`cross_file_first`, control), per target width (1-line, 3-line): **EM rate** and **mean Edit Similarity**, each with **95% percentile bootstrap CIs** (10,000 resamples, over the 400 samples, seeded).\n\n### 4.2 Primary inference (paired, same 400 samples)\n- **EM (binary, paired):** McNemar's test (two-sided for reporting; H1 tested one-sided). Report the discordant-pair table and the paired EM difference.\n- **Edit Similarity (continuous, paired):** Wilcoxon signed-rank (primary, robust) and paired-sample t-test (reported alongside); report mean paired difference and its bootstrap CI.\n- **Effect sizes:** Cohen's h for EM difference; Cohen's d (paired) for Edit-Sim difference. Interpret vs conventional benchmarks (small/medium/large), but emphasize the CI.\n- **H3 interaction:** per-model control-adjusted scores; test whether `(EM_cross \u2212 EM_control)` differs between models (two-sample bootstrap test on the paired deltas).\n\n### 4.3 Significance/power note\nWith N=400 binary paired outcomes, a McNemar test can detect a ~6\u20138% true discordance-rate difference with power \u2265 0.8 (\u03b1=0.05, one-sided) \u2014 this is a planning estimate, not a result; the design reports exact CIs regardless of significance.\n\n### 4.4 Pre-specified error breakdowns (stratified EM and Edit-Sim with CIs)\n- By **context length** (tokens needed: quartiles of total context tokens; plus a \"\u2264256 / 257\u20131024\" GPT-2-feasible bucket).\n- By **number of cross-file context files** (1 / 2\u20134 / >4).\n- By **target-line type** (assignment, `return`, `def`/`class` header, control flow, call/import \u2014 labeled by a lightweight static heuristic, not a full parser; dependency-gated refinement with tree-sitter optional).\n- By **repository** (top-10 largest-repo strata + \"other\").\n- By **gold-line length** (chars).\n- By 1-line vs 3-line condition.\n\n### 4.5 Pre-specified sensitivity checks\n1. **Sampling:** T=0.2, top-p=0.9, 5 seeds; report median \u00b1 IQR of EM/Edit-Sim per model; check ordering stability.\n2. **Truncation policy:** front-clip context to the largest shared token budget (e.g., 512) in both models; report EM delta vs the no-truncation primary \u2014 isolates whether the primary gap is truncation-sensitive.\n3. **Subset seed:** seed 2026 vs seed 0 (same rules); report CI overlap and ranking stability.\n4. **Target width:** 3-line condition vs 1-line.\n5. **Optional:** CodeLlama quantization (e.g., int8) to check 7B results' stability under memory-saving loading.\n\n### 4.6 Reporting contract\nPre-registered tables: (a) eligibility/filter stats, (b) main results table (both conditions, both widths, metrics with CIs), (c) paired-test table, (d) breakdown tables, (e) sensitivity table. Every claim will be phrased conditionally on this protocol; the word \"we ran\" will be avoided; all outputs will be labeled \"simulated under the design\" or \"not yet produced.\"\n\n---\n\n## 5. Feasibility, compute and runtime estimates, provisioning blockers\n\n**Observed environment (verified during exploration):** Python 3.14.4 system; **no** torch/transformers/datasets/numpy/pandas; **no network**; `gpu_enabled: false`; 224 CPUs; ~2 TB RAM; workspace contains only task.json.\n\n**Provisioning blockers (must be resolved before any run):**\n- **Weights:** GPT-2 (\u22480.5 GB) and CodeLlama-7b (\u224813\u201314 GB in bf16) must be present locally \u2014 network is disabled, so they must be provisioned offline into the workspace.\n- **Data:** RepoBench Python `cross_file_first` split must be supplied offline (not present).\n- **Dependencies:** a Python environment with torch + transformers must be installed; Python 3.14 wheel availability for these is **unverified** (flagged). A fallback is to use a compatible pinned Python 3.10/3.11 env, which may itself need provisioning.\n- **Optional evaluator deps:** tree-sitter / sacrebleu for CodeBLEU and type-labeling; gated.\n\n**Compute/runtime estimates (planning figures only, not measured):**\n- GPT-2 small: ~15\u201340 tokens/s/sample on 1 CPU core; for \u2264 ~1k prompt tokens + ~50 output tokens, \u2248 tens of seconds per sample per core; trivially parallelized across 224 cores \u2192 the 400+50 GPT-2 runs complete in minutes.\n- CodeLlama-7B greedy on CPU: ~1\u20135 tokens/s/sample on 1 core (realistically slower; large-model CPU inference), i.e., roughly **1\u201310 min per sample** on a single core. Parallelizing 200 samples across cores (batching on 4\u20138 cores each) gives an estimated **wall-clock range of ~1\u20138 hours** for the 7B side, dependent on achievable throughput \u2014 acknowledged as a wide estimate and a top execution risk (flagged, \u00a76).\n- Memory: 13\u201314 GB for CodeLlama bf16, far below 2 TB; batching of dozens of 7B decodes per node is memory-trivial, so the bottleneck is CPU compute throughput and memory bandwidth, not capacity.\n- 2 TB RAM / 224 cores make the 400-sample 7B run feasible in principle; the design is explicitly CPU-first.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Assumptions and uncertainties (each tagged, with validation/flag)\n\n**All RepoBench-specific items are [ASSUMPTION] \u2014 external knowledge from the RepoBench paper/repo, NOT confirmed by any provided document. Each is validated or flagged as follows:**\n\n1. **[ASSUMPTION-1] Official RepoBench Python test split exists and is retrievable/provisionable.** Validation: researcher confirms the split source; otherwise a seeded split of the full Python set is used and flagged.\n2. **[ASSUMPTION-2] `cross_file_first` sample = gold cross-file context (ordered as provided) + target file prefix to a line-boundary marker; label = next ground-truth line(s); `cross_file_random` = matched random-context control.** Validation: confirm against the official benchmark format/eval script; if the official prompt template (headers/separators/order) differs, lock the design to it and re-derive token budgets. This is the single highest-impact assumption (drives input construction).\n3. **[ASSUMPTION-3] Official scoring contract = Exact Match on next line(s) + Edit Similarity; no execution component in the benchmark's code-completion contract.** Validation: confirm scorer details (set-based vs exact-string matching, whitespace/indent normalization, 1-line vs multi-line target). If the contract differs (e.g., pass@k-style), the metric set changes and the design is revised; as specified, pass@k is explicitly out of scope.\n4. **[ASSUMPTION-4] RepoBench cross-file prompt formatting convention** (header lines, blank-line separators, marker placement). Validation: mirror the official eval script exactly when available; currently flagged as the protocol's own locked convention pending that source.\n5. **[ASSUMPTION-5] \"Next line\" scope (1 line primary / 3 lines secondary) and stop/newline handling.** Validation: researcher confirms the benchmark's target-width convention; default stays 1-line primary.\n6. **[ASSUMPTION-6] Off-the-shelf, no-fine-tuning models define the factor \"size + code specialization.\"** Validation: confirm checkpoints (gpt2, codellama/CodeLlama-7b-hf) are acceptable; no fine-tuning is a fixed design decision.\n7. **[ASSUMPTION-7] The \u22641024-token fitness filter keeps the comparison fair and identical-input; the induced coverage narrowing is a documented tradeoff, not a design flaw.** Validation: report eligibility rate and length-strata difficulty (\u00a73, \u00a74.4); results are scoped to GPT-2-feasible contexts only.\n8. **[ASSUMPTION-8] \"Performance\" = text-match metrics (EM + Edit-Sim); execution correctness is out of scope.** Validation: researcher sign-off on this operational definition; results never phrased as \"code correctness.\"\n9. **[ASSUMPTION-9] CPU-only 7B inference for N\u2248400 (plus qualifier) is feasible (\u22481\u20138 h est.), and Python 3.14/torch wheels install.**\n\n**Non-blocking uncertainties flagged:** exact official separators/template (\u00a72.2), exact scorer normalization (\u00a72.4), eligibility rate of the test split (unknown until data provisioned), and achievable CodeLlama-7B CPU throughput (unknown until measured in the qualifier phase).\n\n---\n\n**END OF DESIGN. No experiment was performed; this document is the complete pre-registered protocol to be executed only after the provisioning blockers in \u00a75 are resolved and the [ASSUMPTION] items in \u00a76 are confirmed or explicitly accepted as assumptions by the researcher.**", "evidence": "Workspace exploration (executed, design-only): /workspace contains only task.json (491 B); its documents field = \"track.md: Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\" Full-filesystem searches (find for track.md, *repobench*, *.md/.csv/.jsonl/.json excluding system dirs) returned only /workspace/task.json. Environment verified: python3 3.14.4 (/usr/bin/python3); importlib.util.find_spec returns False for torch, transformers, datasets, numpy, pandas, git, hf; environment reports network disabled, gpu_enabled false, cpu_affinity_count 224; free -h shows ~2 TB RAM; df shows a 1 TB mount at /workspace (99% used). No datasets, model weights, or ML libraries present anywhere. These observations are consistent with a design-only task; no experiment was run.", "assumptions": ["ASSUMPTION-1: Official RepoBench Python test split exists and can be provisioned offline; otherwise a seeded split of the full Python set is used and flagged.", "ASSUMPTION-2 (highest impact): cross_file_first sample = gold cross-file context (official order) + target-file prefix to a line-boundary marker; label = next gold line(s); cross_file_random = matched random-context control. To be locked to the official format when available.", "ASSUMPTION-3: Official scoring = Exact Match on next line(s) + Edit Similarity; no execution component in the benchmark's code-completion contract.", "ASSUMPTION-4: RepoBench prompt formatting convention (header lines, blank-line separators, marker placement) mirrored from official eval script; currently the protocol's own locked convention pending that source.", "ASSUMPTION-5: 'Next line' scope = 1 line primary / 3 lines secondary with explicit stop/newline handling; default 1-line primary pending researcher confirmation.", "ASSUMPTION-6: Off-the-shelf pretrained gpt2 and codellama/CodeLlama-7b-hf, no fine-tuning, define the factor 'size + code specialization'.", "ASSUMPTION-7: The <=1024-token fitness filter keeps inputs byte-identical for both models; induced coverage narrowing is a documented, reported tradeoff and results are scoped to GPT-2-feasible contexts.", "ASSUMPTION-8: 'Performance' is operationally defined as text-match metrics (EM primary, Edit Similarity secondary); execution-based pass@k correctness is out of scope.", "ASSUMPTION-9: CPU-only CodeLlama-7B inference for ~450 samples is feasible (est. ~1-8h wall clock across 224 cores); Python 3.14 torch/transformers wheel compatibility is unverified and may require a provisioned compatible environment."], "uncertainties": ["Exact official prompt separators/template for RepoBench (not confirmed by any document).", "Exact normalization in the official scorer (whitespace/indent, set-based vs exact-string, 1-line vs 3-line target).", "Eligibility rate of the test split under the 1024-token filter (unknown until data provisioned).", "Achievable CodeLlama-7B CPU token throughput (top execution/feasibility risk; measured in the N=50 qualifier phase).", "Python 3.14 wheel availability for torch/transformers; may force a provisioned alternate environment.", "Tree-sitter/sacrebleu availability for optional CodeBLEU and static line-type labeling (dependency-gated tertiary metrics)."], "stage": "result", "summary": "Deliver final RepoBench GPT-2 vs CodeLlama-7b experiment design"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-28468bfaf5c24707b35839f51ad4d3e5", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "Deliver final RepoBench GPT-2 vs CodeLlama-7b experiment design", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
